This part owns the access boundary: who the app believes opened each connection, and what it
lets them do. app/identity.py, app/routes/actions.py, app/local_cli.py, app/main.py,
app/routes/health.py and app/routes/admin.py are all here TOGETHER and in full, because round 8's
lesson was that a verdict on any one of them needs the others in the same packet.

Answer these, on the code, naming file and symbol:
  - Is a forwarded/forged identity header able to change what the door believes, at ANY gate? The
    unit runs uvicorn with --no-proxy-headers, so the connection's own peer address is what the app
    sees. Trace every place a header could still displace it.
  - app/local_cli.py: the server's own key now EXISTS at /etc/crooks-os/secrets/local_cli_key on
    production (standing fact 2). Round 8's F-05A said `presented` treated an EMPTY
    X-Crooks-Local-Key header as absent, so refusal depended on the header's VALUE rather than its
    PRESENCE. Is that closed, on all three CLI routes, for correct, wrong and empty keys?
  - /whoami now logs `whoami: id=<8 hex> through=... owner=... refusal=...` and returns the same
    token as "check" in its JSON. Confirm the logged id and the returned "check" are the SAME value
    for one request and cannot be confused across concurrent requests.
  - /health now answers callers the owner rule refuses — INCLUDING the server's own loopback curl —
    with limited liveness only: status, build, uptime_s, checks.proxy_identity {ok, detail},
    checks.housekeeping {ok}, limited: true. Confirm the limited document leaks nothing else, and
    that the full document is reachable only by a caller the owner rule admits (or by the server's
    own key). That is round 8's F-NEW-PAD; rule on it here.
  - app/routes/admin.py and the write paths in app/routes/actions.py: does anything in this range
    widen who may reach them?

Remember: label EVERY material finding BLOCKS or FOLLOW-UP, as the first word of the
`finding` field, with one sentence on why. If you cannot tell whether a defect is reachable
on production as configured tonight, say so and label it BLOCKS.

SCHEMA NOTE FOR THIS PART: every finding you return MUST carry a NON-EMPTY `required_repair`
string. If a finding needs no repair — for example a round-8 finding you judge REPAIRED, or a
non-material observation — write the reason in words there, such as "None: repaired at this SHA,
no further work" or "None: observation only". An empty string is rejected and your whole review
is thrown away.
